\section{Bands}{


Each score is cut into \code{n_bands} bands of equal share, tie-safe as in
\code{\link[=scr_bands]{scr_bands()}} (band 1 is the event-richest), unless \code{cuts_a} or \code{cuts_b}
gives the cuts: a numeric vector, or an object from \code{\link[=scr_bands]{scr_bands()}} or
\code{\link[=scr_tiers]{scr_tiers()}}, whose cuts, numbers and labels are then used (the tier
labels for tiers). Bands are left-closed, \code{score >= cut} being the upper
side.

A study given as cuts also sets the objective and the direction of its
score, so the event-rich end of the overlap is the one the study was
fitted with. \code{objective_a}, \code{direction_a} (or their \verb{_b} counterparts)
need not be given then; when given and different from the study, the call
is an error.
}

\section{Cross table}{


One row per pair of bands, every pair listed (empty ones with \code{n = 0}),
then the totals of each band of A (\code{band_b} missing, \code{label_b = "total"}), of each band of B, and the grand total. Per row: \code{n}, \code{pct}
(share of all rows) and, for every outcome, \code{events}, \code{rate} with its
Jeffreys interval \code{rate_lo}, \code{rate_hi} (on the Kish effective size under
weights) and \code{lift} (the rate over the overall rate of that outcome).
With \code{y} the outcome columns have no suffix; with \code{y_a} and \code{y_b} they
end in \verb{_a} and \verb{_b}.
}

\section{Association}{


Spearman's rank correlation (Pearson on mid-ranks, as
\code{cor(method = "spearman")}) and Kendall's tau-b (pairs tied on either
score count neither way, as \code{cor(method = "kendall")}), both on the raw
scores and unweighted. The tau-b counts are exact, by Knight's (1966)
algorithm in \verb{O(n log n)}. \code{oriented} multiplies each estimate by the
signs of the two directions, so it is positive when the two scores put
the same rows at their event-rich ends.
}

\section{Overlap}{


At each depth, each score selects the share \code{depth} of the rows from its
event-rich end (tie-safe: the selection stops at the boundary between two
distinct scores nearest to the target, so the share selected can differ
from \code{depth} by the share of one score value; \code{share_a} and \code{share_b}
